{"step_index":380,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-01T08:13:06Z","thinking":"**Focusing on Animation**\n\nOkay, I'm now zeroing in on using the right tools for animation, given the urgent feedback. It's about bringing the character to life with emotions and gestures, not just a static image. I need to make multiple agents and I need to do it fast. The objective is to make animation with expression, like an expert animator, with multiple agents.\n\n\n**Refining Animation Techniques**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on using the initial character sheet with various expressions, recognizing the need for authentic animation. The prior, single-pose image limited the animation. I'm thinking of slicing the character sheet to extract the needed faces and poses. I am now aware that I will need to be extremely precise in my coordinate selection for cutting up the image, or else it is all wasted effort.\n\n\n**Revisiting Character Creation**\n\nOkay, I'm now revisiting character creation, as I need to use the original AI-generated character sheet. Option 1, procedural drawing, is out. Instead, I'll erase the face of the clean standing character image programmatically. I'll then use `ImageDraw` to draw the different facial expressions. The challenge is matching the style and proportions consistently across scenes, given I must use the AI-generated character.\n\n\n**Analyzing Sprite Mechanics**\n\nI've got a new strategy to create animation by layering different facial expressions and gestures onto the AI-generated \"clean\" sprite, and painting directly onto the image with Python. I'll use a white circle to erase the default face and white rectangles to erase the arms. To ensure accuracy, I plan to analyze the sprite's dimensions, focusing on the head's position. My aim is a perfectly aligned, expressive character animation. I might resort to Python drawing if the coordinate approach fails.\n\n\n**Crafting Hand-Drawn Animation**\n\nMy current focus is on crafting a hand-drawn look for the animation using Python. I've abandoned attempts to reliably use the JPG. The goal is to generate a highly expressive stickman that evokes a hand-drawn feel. I'm exploring a Python script for \"puppet\" rigging. My method involves drawing lines multiple times with slight random offsets to create a rough, sketchy aesthetic, making the animation visually appealing.\n\n\n**Developing Procedural Rigging**\n\nI'm now writing a procedural rigging script from scratch in Python, a departure from the AI-generated flat image. The core concept is creating a sketch-style character with fully independent \"bones\" for expressive movements and facial features, which is essential to create animation. I aim for an expressive, hand-drawn look with a skeletal framework. My focus is on creating dynamic animations, like throwing or smiling.\n\n\n**Implementing Sketch Style**\n\nI'm now implementing the sketch style. I'm leveraging `sketch_line` and `draw_sketch_circle` functions for a hand-drawn feel, drawing multiple lines with random offsets to make a sketchy aesthetic. The skeleton framework is central. This approach will be the basis for my expert stickman.\n\n\n**Refining Facial Expressions**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on fine-tuning the facial expressions for each scene. I'm focusing on the tired, stressed look for scene one, which includes drawing sad eyebrows, a frown, and bags under the eyes to convey the right emotion. I'm also ensuring the eye placement and arcs for the facial features are properly aligned for the sketchy aesthetics.\n\n\n**Generating Scene Variations**\n\nI'm now generating variations for each scene. Scene one involves a tired character with a desk full of books, using `draw_sketch_line` and other functions to create a stressed expression, as I had planned. Scene two will be a confident teacher, and that will be drawn next. The scene variations will provide different animation states. I'm focusing on the overall scene feeling, not just the single character.\n\n\n\n**Crafting Interactive Animations**\n\nI'm now crafting an interactive animation for a new scene! I'm adding an animated pointing arm to the character, using trigonometric functions to calculate its movement. I am also adding a floating pie chart, with a dynamic offset to give it a sense of motion. The focus here is to make the animation interactive.\n\n\n**Simulating Dynamic Movement**\n\nI'm now implementing seesaw physics to simulate dynamic movement. I'm calculating the seesaw's angle and applying trigonometric functions to position its arms, as well as a dropping box. I'm also adding a drawing of a person sitting on the seesaw. My goal is to add interactions.\n\n\n**Refining Expression Techniques**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the expression techniques. I am now creating action for a scene with a character. I have added a wide-open mouth, throwing arms, and a \"glowing\" paper. I've also incorporated the physics of throwing a book. It is about implementing movement and making the animation expressive, and I will be testing these various expressions to make sure they are exactly what I need.\n\n\n**Developing Scene Variety**\n\nI am now developing the scene variety with various emotions. I am working to translate code into specific facial expressions and body language, scene by scene. For Scene 4, I am adding a giant smile and closed eyes. I've implemented a thumbs-up gesture and hip-resting hands. Scene 5 will implement confident action with a thumbs up and a hand on the hip.\n\n\n**Generating Scene Frames**\n\nI'm now generating individual frames for the animation, controlling the flow with a modular design. I am using `draw_expert_stickman` to render the stickman, scene by scene, and have made a progress bar. I am displaying different titles for each frame, and calculating the scene number based on the frame index.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"import sys\nimport os\nimport math\nimport random\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageDraw, ImageFont\n\noutput_dir = r\"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\short_frames\"\nos.makedirs(output_dir, exist_ok=True)\n\nwidth, height = 1080, 1920\nbg_color = \"#FDFBF7\"\nink = \"#111111\"\n\ntry:\n    font_large = ImageFont.truetype(\"arialbd.ttf\", 75)\n    font_med = ImageFont.truetype(\"arial.ttf\", 60)\nexcept:\n    font_large = ImageFont.load_default()\n    font_med = ImageFont.load_default()\n\ndef draw_sketch_line(draw, p1, p2, width=12):\n    # Draw multiple thin lines with slight jitter to mimic handmade ink strokes\n    for _ in range(4):\n        jitter_p1 = (p1[0] + random.randint(-4,4), p1[1] + random.randint(-4,4))\n        jitter_p2 = (p2[0] + random.randint(-4,4), p2[1] + random.randint(-4,4))\n        draw.line([jitter_p1, jitter_p2], fill=ink, width=width-4)\n\ndef draw_sketch_circle(draw, bounding_box, width=12):\n    for _ in range(4):\n        j_box = [\n            bounding_box[0] + random.randint(-4,4),\n            bounding_box[1] + random.randint(-4,4),\n            bounding_box[2] + random.randint(-4,4),\n            bounding_box[3] + random.randint(-4,4)\n        ]\n        draw.arc(j_box, 0, 360, fill=ink, width=width-4)\n\ndef draw_expert_stickman(draw, cx, cy, scene_num, prog):\n    head_r = 90\n    shoulder_y = cy + head_r + 10\n    pelvis_y = shoulder_y + 220\n    \n    # Body\n    draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx, pelvis_y))\n    \n    # Legs (default stance)\n    draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, pelvis_y), (cx-70, pelvis_y+250))\n    draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, pelvis_y), (cx+70, pelvis_y+250))\n    \n    # Head Base (drawn AFTER body so it layers on top)\n    draw.ellipse([cx-head_r, cy-head_r, cx+head_r, cy+head_r], fill=bg_color)\n    draw_sketch_circle(draw, [cx-head_r, cy-head_r, cx+head_r, cy+head_r])\n    \n    if scene_num == 1:\n        # Tired, stressed, bags under eyes\n        draw.line([cx-45, cy-20, cx-15, cy-5], fill=ink, width=8) \n        draw.line([cx+15, cy-5, cx+45, cy-20], fill=ink, width=8) \n        draw.arc([cx-30, cy+30, cx+30, cy+70], 180, 0, fill=ink, width=8) \n        draw.arc([cx-45, cy+10, cx-15, cy+30], 0, 180, fill=ink, width=4)\n        draw.arc([cx+15, cy+10, cx+45, cy+30], 0, 180, fill=ink, width=4)\n        draw.ellipse([cx-35, cy, cx-25, cy+10], fill=ink)\n        draw.ellipse([cx+25, cy, cx+35, cy+10], fill=ink)\n\n        # Hands holding head in despair\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx-130, cy-10))\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx+130, cy-10))\n        \n        # Desk & Books\n        desk_y = pelvis_y + 100\n        draw.rectangle([cx-450, desk_y, cx+450, desk_y+400], fill=\"#bdc3c7\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        num_books = int(1 + prog * 15)\n        for b in range(min(num_books, 10)): \n            bx = cx + 180\n            by = desk_y - 50 - b*50\n            draw.rectangle([bx, by, bx+250, by+50], fill=\"#e74c3c\", outline=ink, width=8)\n\n    elif scene_num == 2:\n        # Confident Teacher\n        draw.arc([cx-45, cy+10, cx+45, cy+60], 0, 180, fill=ink, width=12) \n        draw.ellipse([cx-40, cy-15, cx-20, cy+5], fill=ink) \n        draw.ellipse([cx+20, cy-15, cx+40, cy+5], fill=ink) \n        draw.line([cx-45, cy-35, cx-15, cy-20], fill=ink, width=10)\n        draw.line([cx+15, cy-20, cx+45, cy-35], fill=ink, width=10)\n        \n        # Pointing arm bouncing\n        arm_y = shoulder_y + math.sin(prog * math.pi * 4) * 40\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx-220, arm_y))\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx+100, pelvis_y-50))\n        \n        # Pie chart floating\n        pcx, pcy = 540, cy - 400\n        draw.pieslice([pcx-220, pcy-220, pcx+220, pcy+220], start=0, end=360, fill=\"#ecf0f1\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        offset_x = -25 * math.sin(prog * math.pi)\n        offset_y = -25 * math.sin(prog * math.pi)\n        draw.pieslice([pcx-220+offset_x, pcy-220+offset_y, pcx+220+offset_x, pcy+220+offset_y], start=180, end=252, fill=\"#e74c3c\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        draw.text((pcx+60, pcy-60), \"80%\", fill=ink, font=font_large)\n        draw.text((pcx-140+offset_x, pcy-140+offset_y), \"20%\", fill=bg_color, font=font_med)\n\n    elif scene_num == 3:\n        # Watching intently\n        draw.ellipse([cx-35, cy-10, cx-15, cy+10], fill=ink)\n        draw.ellipse([cx+15, cy-10, cx+35, cy+10], fill=ink)\n        draw.line([cx-25, cy+35, cx+25, cy+35], fill=ink, width=8)\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx-90, pelvis_y))\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx+90, pelvis_y))\n        \n        # Seesaw\n        sw_cx, sw_cy = 540, pelvis_y + 150\n        angle = 20 - prog * 40 \n        L = 380\n        lx = sw_cx - L * math.cos(math.radians(angle))\n        ly = sw_cy - L * math.sin(math.radians(angle))\n        rx = sw_cx + L * math.cos(math.radians(angle))\n        ry = sw_cy + L * math.sin(math.radians(angle))\n        draw.polygon([(sw_cx, sw_cy), (sw_cx-50, sw_cy+120), (sw_cx+50, sw_cy+120)], fill=ink)\n        draw.line([lx, ly, rx, ry], fill=ink, width=30)\n        drop_y = ly - 450 + (prog**3) * 450\n        draw.rectangle([lx-90, min(drop_y, ly-180), lx+90, min(drop_y+180, ly)], fill=\"#3498db\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        draw.text((lx-70, min(drop_y+60, ly-110)), \"20%\", fill=bg_color, font=font_med)\n        draw.rectangle([rx-170, ry-340, rx+170, ry], fill=\"#e74c3c\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        draw.text((rx-70, ry-200), \"80%\", fill=bg_color, font=font_large)\n\n    elif scene_num == 4:\n        # Dynamic Action / Shouting\n        draw.arc([cx-45, cy-10, cx+45, cy+60], 0, 180, fill=ink, width=15)\n        draw.line([cx-35, cy+25, cx+35, cy+25], fill=bg_color, width=8) # Teeth\n        draw.ellipse([cx-45, cy-25, cx-25, cy-5], fill=ink)\n        draw.ellipse([cx+25, cy-25, cx+45, cy-5], fill=ink)\n        \n        # Throw and hold\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx-220, shoulder_y-70)) \n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx+200, shoulder_y-120)) \n        \n        book_x = cx - 280 - prog * 650\n        book_y = shoulder_y - 70 - prog * 250 + prog**2 * 900\n        draw.rectangle([book_x, book_y, book_x+140, book_y+180], fill=\"#95a5a6\", outline=ink, width=10)\n        \n        # Glowing paper\n        draw.rectangle([cx+100, shoulder_y-280, cx+280, shoulder_y-30], fill=\"#f1c40f\", outline=ink, width=12)\n        draw.text((cx+120, shoulder_y-200), \"PYQs\", fill=ink, font=font_large)\n        gl = prog * 15\n        draw.line([cx+190, shoulder_y-300, cx+190, shoulder_y-350+gl], fill=\"#f39c12\", width=10)\n        draw.line([cx+300, shoulder_y-155, cx+350-gl, shoulder_y-155], fill=\"#f39c12\", width=10)\n\n    elif scene_num == 5:\n        # Extreme Thumbs up\n        draw.arc([cx-55, cy-15, cx+55, cy+65], 0, 180, fill=ink, width=14) \n        draw.arc([cx-50, cy-35, cx-15, cy-15], 180, 0, fill=ink, width=12) \n        draw.arc([cx+15, cy-35, cx+50, cy-15], 180, 0, fill=ink, width=12) \n        \n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx-200, shoulder_y-70)) \n        draw.ellipse([cx-250, shoulder_y-120, cx-180, shoulder_y-50], fill=ink) \n        draw.line([cx-215, shoulder_y-120, cx-215, shoulder_y-170], fill=ink, width=18) \n        \n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx, shoulder_y), (cx+120, shoulder_y+120))\n        draw_sketch_line(draw, (cx+120, shoulder_y+120), (cx+50, pelvis_y+20))\n\ndef draw_frame(f_idx, total_frames):\n    img = Image.new(\"RGB\", (width, height), bg_color)\n    draw = ImageDraw.Draw(img)\n    \n    scene_duration = 270 \n    scene_num = min((f_idx // scene_duration) + 1, 5)\n    scene_frame = f_idx % scene_duration\n    prog = scene_frame / float(scene_duration)\n    \n    titles = [\n        \"STUDYING FOR HOURS?\",\n        \"THE 80/20 RULE\",\n        \"80% RESULTS = 20% EFFORT\",\n        \"FOCUS ON PYQs & CORE\",\n        \"WORK SMART, NOT HARD\"\n    ]\n    \n    text = titles[scene_num-1]\n    bbox = font_large.getbbox(text)\n    tw = bbox[2] - bbox[0]\n    draw.text(((width - tw)/2, 250), text, fill=ink, font=font_large)\n    \n    draw.rectangle([0, height-20, width * (f_idx / float(total_frames)), height], fill=\"#e74c3c\")\n    \n    # Fully articulated, highly expressive, handmade-style rigged character\n    draw_expert_stickman(draw, 540, 1050, scene_num, prog)\n    \n    return img\n\ntotal = 1350\nstart_f = int(sys.argv[1])\nend_f = int(sys.argv[2])\n\nfor i in range(start_f, end_f + 1):\n    frame = draw_frame(i, total)\n    filename = f\"frame_{i:04d}.png\"\n    frame.save(os.path.join(output_dir, filename))\n","Description":"Writing procedural expert animator sketch script for dynamic rigging","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\short_animation_expert.py","toolAction":"Writing the expert animation script with procedural sketching and dynamic rigging","toolSummary":"Write Expert Script"}}]}
